Upstream-bound: no
Subject: [PORT_HOST] battle Digimon change: the wireframe links its lines with the 64-bit pointer

FIGHTSTG_drawMeshWireframe (battle_mode/model_mesh.c) draws the old Digimon as
a green wireframe while a Digimon change plays (FIGHTSTG_updateDigimonChange,
the animation of the battle's Digivolve command: startChange turns the model's
second layer on as BATTLE_LAYER_WIREFRAME). Each line is linked into the layer's
OT by hand, with linkTag(tag, addr), which keeps the top byte of the old tag
word and puts a 24-bit PS1 address under it
(`(u_long)state.prim.ptr & 0xFFFFFF`). Under PSY-Z the tag word is the whole
next-primitive pointer (P_TAG is {u_long addr; u_long len}), so the OT entry
and every line's tag got the low 32 bits of a pointer with its top byte mixed
in, e.g. 0x56ff5b20 for the primitive at 0x555556ff5b20. DrawOTag then walked
into that address when it drew the layer: GPU_Enqueue (libgpu.c:171) read
env->len of a wild pointer, "CRASH user exception ... data abort, translation
fault" on the Switch and a SIGSEGV on the PC host, a few frames after the
Digimon change starts (the Digimon change is the only user of the wireframe
layer; the Rookie/Champion automatic digivolution of the story uses another
task). 0039 made drawMesh index the OT with the OT_TYPE stride; this was the
one mesh routine that was never ported.

Under PORT_HOST linkTag returns the address it is given and the lines link the
full primitive pointer (WIRE_PRIM_ADDR); the PS1 build keeps the 24-bit
expression the match needs.

Test hook (same hunk group as DW3_FORCE_FORM / DW3_FORCE_NICKNAME of 0064, PC
only, an environment variable, nothing happens when unset):
DW3_FORCE_DIGI_SLOTS=<id>[,<id>[,<id>]] gives the first partner those Digimon
as entries and battle slots, so the battle's Digivolve command lists them
without playing up to a digivolution. Used by the replay scenarios
`us|eu-battle-digivolve-command` (docs/P6.15_REPLAY_TESTS.md).

Two log lines, once per Digimon change, tell an OLED / replay run that the
wireframe layer was drawn and the change finished (`digimon_change.c`, both
under PORT_HOST):
`dw3-battle: digimon change wireframe starts (to Digimon id N, digivolve command|blast)`
`dw3-battle: digimon change done, back to the battle (Digimon id N)`

--- a/src/battle/battle_mode/model_mesh.c
+++ b/src/battle/battle_mode/model_mesh.c
@@ -913,11 +913,24 @@
    expression, sched1 moves the tag's mask after the address's, which swaps
    their registers. */
 static inline u_long linkTag(u_long tag, u_long addr) {
+#if PORT_HOST
+    /* PSY-Z's tag word is the whole next-primitive pointer (P_TAG.addr is a
+       u_long), not a 24-bit PS1 address under a top byte of its own */
+    (void)tag;
+    return addr;
+#else
     u_long word = tag & 0xFF000000;
     word |= addr;
     return word;
+#endif
 }
 
+#if PORT_HOST
+#define WIRE_PRIM_ADDR(p) ((u_long)(p))
+#else
+#define WIRE_PRIM_ADDR(p) ((u_long)(p) & 0xFFFFFF)
+#endif
+
 /* Draws a Mesh as wireframe: each polygon of its command runs as a green
    LINE_F4 through its screen points, with a LINE_F2 to close a quad. Each
    addPrim is written out with linkTag. */
@@ -1004,7 +1017,7 @@
                     tag = *state.prim.tag;
                     *state.prim.tag = linkTag(tag, getaddr(state.ot));
                     tag = *state.ot;
-                    *state.ot = linkTag(tag, (u_long)state.prim.ptr & 0xFFFFFF);
+                    *state.ot = linkTag(tag, WIRE_PRIM_ADDR(state.prim.ptr));
                     state.prim.lineF4++;
                     if (state.quad) {
                         setLineF2(state.prim.lineF2);
@@ -1014,7 +1027,7 @@
                         tag = *state.prim.tag;
                         *state.prim.tag = linkTag(tag, getaddr(state.ot));
                         tag = *state.ot;
-                        *state.ot = linkTag(tag, (u_long)state.prim.ptr & 0xFFFFFF);
+                        *state.ot = linkTag(tag, WIRE_PRIM_ADDR(state.prim.ptr));
                         state.prim.lineF2++;
                     }
                     n = state.quad + 3;
